window.PROBLEMS=[{"id":"sql-d01-delete-duplicate-emails","day":1,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 196","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Delete Duplicate Emails","statement":"The `person` table has one row per sign-up. The same email can appear on more than one row.\n\nWrite one `DELETE` statement that removes the duplicates. For each email, keep the row with the **smallest `id`** and delete the rest.\n\nAfter your statement runs, the checker reads the whole `person` table and compares it with the expected rows.","starter":"-- Write one DELETE statement.\n","hints":["Two rows are duplicates when they share an email. Before you write any SQL, decide which single row per email you will keep.","GROUP BY email gives one group per email. Inside each group, MIN(id) picks the row to keep.","Collect the ids to keep with a subquery, then delete every row whose id is not in that list."],"approach":"The grain of the answer is **one kept row per email**. A subquery with `GROUP BY email` and `MIN(id)` lists exactly the ids to keep. Everything else goes.\n\n`NOT IN` is safe here because `id` is never NULL. If the column could hold NULLs, `NOT IN` would match nothing, and you would switch to `NOT EXISTS` or a `DELETE ... USING` self-join.\n\nSay the tie rule out loud before you code: \"smallest id wins\". Interviewers listen for it.\n\n```sql\nDELETE FROM person\nWHERE id NOT IN (SELECT MIN(id) FROM person GROUP BY email)\n```","kind":"mutate","ordered":false,"verify":"SELECT id, email FROM person ORDER BY id","schema":["person(id INT, email VARCHAR)   -- id is unique"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE person(id INT, email VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO person VALUES (1, 'a@x.com'), (2, 'b@x.com'), (3, 'a@x.com')"],"tables":[{"name":"person","cols":"id INT, email VARCHAR","rows":[[1,"a@x.com"],[2,"b@x.com"],[3,"a@x.com"]]}],"expected":{"columns":["id","email"],"rows":[[1,"a@x.com"],[2,"b@x.com"]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE person(id INT, email VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO person VALUES (1, 'a'), (2, 'b')"],"expected":{"columns":["id","email"],"rows":[[1,"a"],[2,"b"]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE person(id INT, email VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO person VALUES (5, 'z'), (2, 'z'), (9, 'z'), (4, 'y')"],"expected":{"columns":["id","email"],"rows":[[2,"z"],[4,"y"]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE person(id INT, email VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO person VALUES (1, 'a'), (2, 'a'), (3, 'b'), (4, 'b'), (5, 'c')"],"expected":{"columns":["id","email"],"rows":[[1,"a"],[3,"b"],[5,"c"]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE person(id INT, email VARCHAR)"],"expected":{"columns":["id","email"],"rows":[]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d02-department-top-three-salaries","day":2,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 185","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Department Top Three Salaries","statement":"Find the top earners in every department. An employee is a top earner if their salary is one of the **three highest distinct salaries** in their department.\n\nIf two people share a salary, both are included, and that salary uses only one of the three places.\n\nReturn the columns `department`, `employee` and `salary`. Departments with no employees do not appear.","starter":"-- Return: department, employee, salary\nSELECT\n","hints":["You need a rank inside each department, so think \"window function with PARTITION BY\".","Decide the tie rule first. \"Three highest distinct salaries\" means two people on the same salary share one place and the next salary gets the next place. Which of RANK, DENSE_RANK and ROW_NUMBER does that?","Compute DENSE_RANK() over each department ordered by salary descending in a subquery, then keep rows where the rank is 3 or less."],"approach":"The rule \"distinct salaries, ties share a place\" is exactly `DENSE_RANK`. `RANK` skips numbers after a tie (1, 1, 3), so the person who is third by salary would be left out. `ROW_NUMBER` never ties, so it drops people who earn the same.\n\nA window function cannot be filtered in the same `SELECT`, so the rank goes in a subquery and the outer query filters `rnk \u003c= 3`. `QUALIFY` does the same in one step in DuckDB.\n\nSay the tie rule out loud before you pick the function. That sentence is the whole interview signal.\n\n```sql\nSELECT department, employee, salary\nFROM (\n  SELECT d.name AS department, e.name AS employee, e.salary,\n         DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY e.department_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employee e\n  JOIN department d ON d.id = e.department_id\n)\nWHERE rnk \u003c= 3\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["department(id INT, name VARCHAR)","employee(id INT, name VARCHAR, salary INT, department_id INT)"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE department(id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO department VALUES (1, 'Sales'), (2, 'Tech')","CREATE TABLE employee(id INT, name VARCHAR, salary INT, department_id INT)","INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1, 'Ann', 90, 1), (2, 'Bob', 80, 1), (3, 'Cy', 70, 1), (4, 'Di', 60, 1), (5, 'Eve', 100, 2), (6, 'Fay', 95, 2)"],"tables":[{"name":"department","cols":"id INT, name VARCHAR","rows":[[1,"Sales"],[2,"Tech"]]},{"name":"employee","cols":"id INT, name VARCHAR, salary INT, department_id INT","rows":[[1,"Ann",90,1],[2,"Bob",80,1],[3,"Cy",70,1],[4,"Di",60,1],[5,"Eve",100,2],[6,"Fay",95,2]]}],"expected":{"columns":["department","employee","salary"],"rows":[["Sales","Ann",90],["Sales","Bob",80],["Sales","Cy",70],["Tech","Eve",100],["Tech","Fay",95]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE department(id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO department VALUES (1, 'Sales'), (2, 'Tech')","CREATE TABLE employee(id INT, name VARCHAR, salary INT, department_id INT)","INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1, 'A', 100, 1), (2, 'B', 100, 1), (3, 'C', 90, 1), (4, 'D', 80, 1), (5, 'E', 70, 1), (6, 'F', 10, 2)"],"expected":{"columns":["department","employee","salary"],"rows":[["Sales","A",100],["Sales","B",100],["Sales","C",90],["Sales","D",80],["Tech","F",10]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE department(id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO department VALUES (1, 'Sales'), (2, 'Tech'), (3, 'Empty')","CREATE TABLE employee(id INT, name VARCHAR, salary INT, department_id INT)","INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1, 'P', 50, 1), (2, 'Q', 40, 1), (3, 'R', 30, 2), (4, 'S', 20, 2)"],"expected":{"columns":["department","employee","salary"],"rows":[["Sales","P",50],["Sales","Q",40],["Tech","R",30],["Tech","S",20]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE department(id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO department VALUES (1, 'Ops')","CREATE TABLE employee(id INT, name VARCHAR, salary INT, department_id INT)","INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1, 'W', 10, 1), (2, 'X', 10, 1), (3, 'Y', 10, 1), (4, 'Z', 10, 1)"],"expected":{"columns":["department","employee","salary"],"rows":[["Ops","W",10],["Ops","X",10],["Ops","Y",10],["Ops","Z",10]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d03-consecutive-numbers","day":3,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 180","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Consecutive Numbers","statement":"Read the `logs` table in order of `id`. Find every number that shows up **at least three times in a row**: three neighbouring rows with the same `num`.\n\nReturn each such number once, in a column named `num`. If no number qualifies, return no rows.","starter":"-- Return: num\nSELECT\n","hints":["\"In a row\" means you need to compare a row with the rows next to it. Which window functions look at a neighbour?","LAG(num, 1) is the previous number and LAG(num, 2) is the one before that. Order both by id.","Keep the rows where num equals both of those, and use DISTINCT because a run of four would match twice."],"approach":"Two `LAG` calls bring the previous two values onto the current row. A row that equals both is the end of a run of at least three. A run of four has two such rows, so `DISTINCT` is needed.\n\nThe same idea works with a self-join on `id`, `id + 1` and `id + 2`. That version breaks if ids have gaps, while `LAG` follows row order. Name which one you chose and why.\n\n```sql\nSELECT DISTINCT num\nFROM (\n  SELECT num,\n         LAG(num, 1) OVER (ORDER BY id) AS prev1,\n         LAG(num, 2) OVER (ORDER BY id) AS prev2\n  FROM logs\n)\nWHERE num = prev1 AND num = prev2\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["logs(id INT, num INT)   -- read the rows in order of id"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE logs(id INT, num INT)","INSERT INTO logs VALUES (1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 1), (4, 2), (5, 1), (6, 2), (7, 2)"],"tables":[{"name":"logs","cols":"id INT, num INT","rows":[[1,1],[2,1],[3,1],[4,2],[5,1],[6,2],[7,2]]}],"expected":{"columns":["num"],"rows":[[1]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE logs(id INT, num INT)","INSERT INTO logs VALUES (1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 2), (4, 1), (5, 1)"],"expected":{"columns":["num"],"rows":[]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE logs(id INT, num INT)","INSERT INTO logs VALUES (1, 5), (2, 5), (3, 5), (4, 5)"],"expected":{"columns":["num"],"rows":[[5]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE logs(id INT, num INT)","INSERT INTO logs VALUES (1, 3), (2, 3), (3, 3), (4, 4), (5, 4), (6, 4), (7, 4), (8, 9)"],"expected":{"columns":["num"],"rows":[[3],[4]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE logs(id INT, num INT)","INSERT INTO logs VALUES (1, 7)"],"expected":{"columns":["num"],"rows":[]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d04-immediate-food-delivery","day":4,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 1174","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Immediate Food Delivery II","statement":"An order is **immediate** when `order_date` equals the customer's `preferred_date`. Otherwise it is scheduled.\n\nA customer's **first order** is their order with the earliest `order_date`. Each customer has only one order on that earliest date.\n\nReturn the percentage of immediate orders among the first orders of all customers, rounded to 2 decimals, in a column named `immediate_percentage`.","starter":"-- Return: immediate_percentage\nSELECT\n","hints":["First answer a smaller question: which single row is each customer's first order? Write that query alone and check it.","A correlated subquery, or a window function ranked by order_date, finds that row for each customer.","Once only first orders remain, the percentage is the average of a 1-or-0 flag times 100."],"approach":"State the grain first: **one row per customer, their first order**. Filter to that row, then the percentage is `AVG(flag) * 100`, where the flag is 1 for an immediate order and 0 otherwise.\n\nA window version works too: `RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date) = 1`. The correlated subquery is shorter when each customer has one order on their first date, which the question promises.\n\nCheck your rounding on a case like 1 of 3, where the answer is 33.33 and not 33.333333.\n\n```sql\nSELECT ROUND(100.0 * AVG(CASE WHEN d.order_date = d.preferred_date THEN 1 ELSE 0 END), 2) AS immediate_percentage\nFROM delivery d\nWHERE d.order_date = (\n  SELECT MIN(order_date) FROM delivery WHERE customer_id = d.customer_id\n)\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["delivery(delivery_id INT, customer_id INT, order_date DATE, preferred_date DATE)"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE delivery(delivery_id INT, customer_id INT, order_date DATE, preferred_date DATE)","INSERT INTO delivery VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2019-08-01', DATE '2019-08-02'), (2, 2, DATE '2019-08-02', DATE '2019-08-02'), (3, 1, DATE '2019-08-11', DATE '2019-08-12'), (4, 3, DATE '2019-08-24', DATE '2019-08-24'), (5, 3, DATE '2019-08-21', DATE '2019-08-22'), (6, 2, DATE '2019-08-11', DATE '2019-08-13'), (7, 4, DATE '2019-08-09', DATE '2019-08-09')"],"tables":[{"name":"delivery","cols":"delivery_id INT, customer_id INT, order_date DATE, preferred_date DATE","rows":[[1,1,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-01'"},{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-02'"}],[2,2,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-02'"},{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-02'"}],[3,1,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-11'"},{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-12'"}],[4,3,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-24'"},{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-24'"}],[5,3,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-21'"},{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-22'"}],[6,2,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-11'"},{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-13'"}],[7,4,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-09'"},{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-09'"}]]}],"expected":{"columns":["immediate_percentage"],"rows":[[50]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE delivery(delivery_id INT, customer_id INT, order_date DATE, preferred_date DATE)","INSERT INTO delivery VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2020-01-01', DATE '2020-01-01'), (2, 2, DATE '2020-01-02', DATE '2020-01-03'), (3, 3, DATE '2020-01-04', DATE '2020-01-05')"],"expected":{"columns":["immediate_percentage"],"rows":[[33.33]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE delivery(delivery_id INT, customer_id INT, order_date DATE, preferred_date DATE)","INSERT INTO delivery VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2020-01-01', DATE '2020-01-01'), (2, 2, DATE '2020-01-02', DATE '2020-01-02')"],"expected":{"columns":["immediate_percentage"],"rows":[[100]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE delivery(delivery_id INT, customer_id INT, order_date DATE, preferred_date DATE)","INSERT INTO delivery VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2020-01-01', DATE '2020-01-02'), (2, 2, DATE '2020-01-03', DATE '2020-01-04')"],"expected":{"columns":["immediate_percentage"],"rows":[[0]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE delivery(delivery_id INT, customer_id INT, order_date DATE, preferred_date DATE)","INSERT INTO delivery VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2020-01-01', DATE '2020-01-05'), (2, 1, DATE '2020-01-09', DATE '2020-01-09')"],"expected":{"columns":["immediate_percentage"],"rows":[[0]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d05-first-year-sales","day":5,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 1070","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Product Sales Analysis III","statement":"For every product, return **all** of its sales rows from the **first year** that product was sold. A product can have several sales in its first year, and each one is returned.\n\nReturn the columns `product_id`, `first_year`, `quantity` and `price`.","starter":"-- Return: product_id, first_year, quantity, price\nSELECT\n","hints":["Say the grain of the answer out loud: it is rows of sales, not one row per product.","You need each product's earliest year attached to every one of its rows. A window MIN with PARTITION BY product_id does that.","Keep the rows where year equals that earliest year."],"approach":"The grain is **sales rows from each product's first year**. First attach the product's earliest year to every row, then filter on equality. A window `MIN(year) OVER (PARTITION BY product_id)` does both in one pass.\n\nThe same result comes from `WHERE (product_id, year) IN (SELECT product_id, MIN(year) FROM sales GROUP BY product_id)`.\n\n`ROW_NUMBER() = 1` looks right but returns a single row per product, which loses the second sale in a shared first year.\n\n```sql\nSELECT product_id, year AS first_year, quantity, price\nFROM (\n  SELECT *, MIN(year) OVER (PARTITION BY product_id) AS y0\n  FROM sales\n)\nWHERE year = y0\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["sales(sale_id INT, product_id INT, year INT, quantity INT, price INT)"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE sales(sale_id INT, product_id INT, year INT, quantity INT, price INT)","INSERT INTO sales VALUES (1, 100, 2008, 10, 5000), (2, 100, 2009, 12, 5000), (7, 200, 2011, 15, 9000)"],"tables":[{"name":"sales","cols":"sale_id INT, product_id INT, year INT, quantity INT, price INT","rows":[[1,100,2008,10,5000],[2,100,2009,12,5000],[7,200,2011,15,9000]]}],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","first_year","quantity","price"],"rows":[[100,2008,10,5000],[200,2011,15,9000]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE sales(sale_id INT, product_id INT, year INT, quantity INT, price INT)","INSERT INTO sales VALUES (1, 100, 2008, 10, 5000), (2, 100, 2008, 5, 5100), (3, 100, 2010, 7, 5000)"],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","first_year","quantity","price"],"rows":[[100,2008,10,5000],[100,2008,5,5100]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE sales(sale_id INT, product_id INT, year INT, quantity INT, price INT)","INSERT INTO sales VALUES (1, 100, 2012, 3, 10), (2, 100, 2010, 4, 11), (3, 200, 2011, 8, 20), (4, 200, 2013, 9, 21)"],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","first_year","quantity","price"],"rows":[[100,2010,4,11],[200,2011,8,20]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE sales(sale_id INT, product_id INT, year INT, quantity INT, price INT)","INSERT INTO sales VALUES (1, 300, 2015, 1, 99)"],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","first_year","quantity","price"],"rows":[[300,2015,1,99]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d06-price-on-a-date","day":6,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 1164","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Product Price at a Given Date","statement":"Every product starts at a price of **10**. Each row of `price_changes` says that on `change_date` the product's price became `new_price`.\n\nReturn the price of **every product** on **2019-08-16**, with the columns `product_id` and `price`. A change dated 2019-08-16 already applies on that day.\n\nProducts whose first change comes after that date still appear, at the starting price of 10.","starter":"-- Return: product_id, price  (as of 2019-08-16)\nSELECT\n","hints":["This is a \"what was true on this date\" lookup. For each product you want the most recent change on or before 2019-08-16.","Filter the changes to change_date \u003c= 2019-08-16, then pick the newest row per product with ROW_NUMBER ordered by change_date descending.","Start from the list of all products and LEFT JOIN the picked rows, so products with no change yet keep a row. COALESCE the missing price to 10."],"approach":"This is the slowly-changing-dimension idea in miniature: **the latest row at or before a point in time**. Filter to `change_date \u003c= :date`, rank per product by `change_date DESC`, keep rank 1.\n\nProducts with no row before the date would vanish from an inner join. Start from the distinct product list and `LEFT JOIN`, then `COALESCE(price, 10)` for the default.\n\nWatch the boundary: `\u003c=` includes a change made on the day itself. Say which one you chose.\n\n```sql\nSELECT p.product_id, COALESCE(x.new_price, 10) AS price\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT product_id FROM price_changes) p\nLEFT JOIN (\n  SELECT product_id, new_price,\n         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY change_date DESC) AS rn\n  FROM price_changes WHERE change_date \u003c= DATE '2019-08-16'\n) x ON x.product_id = p.product_id AND x.rn = 1\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["price_changes(product_id INT, new_price INT, change_date DATE)"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE price_changes(product_id INT, new_price INT, change_date DATE)","INSERT INTO price_changes VALUES (1, 20, DATE '2019-08-14'), (2, 50, DATE '2019-08-14'), (1, 30, DATE '2019-08-15'), (1, 35, DATE '2019-08-16'), (2, 65, DATE '2019-08-17'), (3, 20, DATE '2019-08-18')"],"tables":[{"name":"price_changes","cols":"product_id INT, new_price INT, change_date DATE","rows":[[1,20,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-14'"}],[2,50,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-14'"}],[1,30,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-15'"}],[1,35,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-16'"}],[2,65,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-17'"}],[3,20,{"sql":"DATE '2019-08-18'"}]]}],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","price"],"rows":[[1,35],[2,50],[3,10]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE price_changes(product_id INT, new_price INT, change_date DATE)","INSERT INTO price_changes VALUES (4, 40, DATE '2019-08-01'), (4, 25, DATE '2019-08-10')"],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","price"],"rows":[[4,25]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE price_changes(product_id INT, new_price INT, change_date DATE)","INSERT INTO price_changes VALUES (5, 99, DATE '2019-09-01')"],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","price"],"rows":[[5,10]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE price_changes(product_id INT, new_price INT, change_date DATE)","INSERT INTO price_changes VALUES (6, 12, DATE '2018-01-01'), (6, 18, DATE '2019-08-16'), (7, 70, DATE '2019-08-17'), (7, 71, DATE '2019-12-01')"],"expected":{"columns":["product_id","price"],"rows":[[6,18],[7,10]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d08-stadium-traffic","day":8,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 601","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Human Traffic of Stadium","statement":"Each row is one day at a stadium. Find the days that belong to a **run of three or more consecutive ids** where **every day had at least 100 people**.\n\nConsecutive means the ids follow each other with no gap. A day with fewer than 100 people, or a missing id, ends a run.\n\nReturn `id`, `visit_date` and `people`, ordered by `visit_date` ascending.","starter":"-- Return: id, visit_date, people  (ordered by visit_date)\nSELECT\n","hints":["This is a \"gaps and islands\" problem: you want groups of neighbouring ids that all pass the people filter.","Keep only rows with people >= 100 first. Then id minus ROW_NUMBER() over those rows is the same number for every id in one unbroken run.","Group by that number, keep the groups with at least three rows, and join back to list their days."],"approach":"After you filter to busy days, `id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id)` stays constant while ids are consecutive and jumps when an id is missing or a quiet day was removed. That constant is a label for each run.\n\nCount rows per label, keep labels with three or more, and return those rows ordered by date.\n\nThe order matters: filter **before** you number the rows. Numbering the whole table first hides the gaps you are trying to find.\n\n```sql\nWITH busy AS (\n  SELECT *, id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id) AS grp\n  FROM stadium\n  WHERE people >= 100\n)\nSELECT id, visit_date, people\nFROM busy\nWHERE grp IN (SELECT grp FROM busy GROUP BY grp HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3)\nORDER BY visit_date\n```","kind":"select","ordered":true,"schema":["stadium(id INT, visit_date DATE, people INT)   -- id and visit_date are unique"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE stadium(id INT, visit_date DATE, people INT)","INSERT INTO stadium VALUES (1, DATE '2017-01-01', 10), (2, DATE '2017-01-02', 109), (3, DATE '2017-01-03', 150), (4, DATE '2017-01-04', 99), (5, DATE '2017-01-05', 145), (6, DATE '2017-01-06', 1455), (7, DATE '2017-01-07', 199), (8, DATE '2017-01-09', 188)"],"tables":[{"name":"stadium","cols":"id INT, visit_date DATE, people INT","rows":[[1,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-01'"},10],[2,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-02'"},109],[3,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-03'"},150],[4,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-04'"},99],[5,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-05'"},145],[6,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-06'"},1455],[7,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-07'"},199],[8,{"sql":"DATE '2017-01-09'"},188]]}],"expected":{"columns":["id","visit_date","people"],"rows":[[5,"2017-01-05",145],[6,"2017-01-06",1455],[7,"2017-01-07",199],[8,"2017-01-09",188]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE stadium(id INT, visit_date DATE, people INT)","INSERT INTO stadium VALUES (1, DATE '2020-01-01', 100), (2, DATE '2020-01-02', 100), (4, DATE '2020-01-04', 100), (5, DATE '2020-01-05', 100)"],"expected":{"columns":["id","visit_date","people"],"rows":[]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE stadium(id INT, visit_date DATE, people INT)","INSERT INTO stadium VALUES (1, DATE '2020-01-01', 100), (2, DATE '2020-01-02', 120), (3, DATE '2020-01-03', 130), (4, DATE '2020-01-04', 99)"],"expected":{"columns":["id","visit_date","people"],"rows":[[1,"2020-01-01",100],[2,"2020-01-02",120],[3,"2020-01-03",130]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE stadium(id INT, visit_date DATE, people INT)","INSERT INTO stadium VALUES (1, DATE '2020-02-01', 200), (2, DATE '2020-02-02', 200), (3, DATE '2020-02-03', 50), (4, DATE '2020-02-04', 300), (5, DATE '2020-02-05', 301), (6, DATE '2020-02-06', 302)"],"expected":{"columns":["id","visit_date","people"],"rows":[[4,"2020-02-04",300],[5,"2020-02-05",301],[6,"2020-02-06",302]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d09-last-person-on-the-bus","day":9,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 1204","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Last Person to Fit in the Bus","statement":"People board a bus one at a time, in order of `turn`. The bus can carry **1000 kg** in total. Someone boards only if the total weight on the bus, including them, stays at or under 1000.\n\nBoarding stops at the first person who would push it over. Return the name of the **last person who boards**, in a column named `person_name`. The first person in line always fits.","starter":"-- Return: person_name\nSELECT\n","hints":["You need the total weight on the bus after each person boards. That is a running total.","SUM(weight) OVER (ORDER BY turn) gives the total up to and including each row.","Keep the rows whose running total is 1000 or less, then take the one with the largest total."],"approach":"A running total is a windowed `SUM` ordered by the boarding order: `SUM(weight) OVER (ORDER BY turn)`. Because every weight is positive, the total only goes up, so the last person under the limit is the one with the **largest total that is still 1000 or less**.\n\nSay the boundary out loud: exactly 1000 kg still fits, so the test is `\u003c=`.\n\n```sql\nSELECT person_name\nFROM (\n  SELECT person_name, SUM(weight) OVER (ORDER BY turn) AS total\n  FROM queue\n)\nWHERE total \u003c= 1000\nORDER BY total DESC\nLIMIT 1\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["queue(person_id INT, person_name VARCHAR, weight INT, turn INT)   -- turn is unique"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE queue(person_id INT, person_name VARCHAR, weight INT, turn INT)","INSERT INTO queue VALUES (5, 'Alice', 250, 4), (4, 'Bob', 175, 1), (3, 'Alex', 350, 2), (6, 'John', 400, 3), (1, 'Winston', 500, 6), (2, 'Marie', 200, 5)"],"tables":[{"name":"queue","cols":"person_id INT, person_name VARCHAR, weight INT, turn INT","rows":[[5,"Alice",250,4],[4,"Bob",175,1],[3,"Alex",350,2],[6,"John",400,3],[1,"Winston",500,6],[2,"Marie",200,5]]}],"expected":{"columns":["person_name"],"rows":[["John"]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE queue(person_id INT, person_name VARCHAR, weight INT, turn INT)","INSERT INTO queue VALUES (1, 'A', 400, 1), (2, 'B', 300, 2), (3, 'C', 300, 3), (4, 'D', 1, 4)"],"expected":{"columns":["person_name"],"rows":[["C"]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE queue(person_id INT, person_name VARCHAR, weight INT, turn INT)","INSERT INTO queue VALUES (1, 'A', 100, 1), (2, 'B', 100, 2), (3, 'C', 100, 3)"],"expected":{"columns":["person_name"],"rows":[["C"]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE queue(person_id INT, person_name VARCHAR, weight INT, turn INT)","INSERT INTO queue VALUES (9, 'A', 900, 1), (8, 'B', 200, 2), (7, 'C', 50, 3)"],"expected":{"columns":["person_name"],"rows":[["A"]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d10-ride-cancellation-rate","day":10,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 262","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Trips and Users","statement":"A ride is **cancelled** when its status is `cancelled_by_driver` or `cancelled_by_rider`. It is **completed** otherwise.\n\nFor each day from **2013-10-01 to 2013-10-03**, find the cancellation rate: cancelled rides divided by all rides on that day, rounded to 2 decimals.\n\nCount a ride only if **neither** its rider **nor** its driver is banned. Days with no counted rides do not appear.\n\nReturn `ride_date` and `cancellation_rate`.","starter":"-- Return: ride_date, cancellation_rate\nSELECT\n","hints":["Two things shrink the set of rides before you count: banned people and the date window. Decide which rides are \"in\" first.","Join members twice, once as the rider and once as the driver, and require banned = 'No' on both joins.","After grouping by day, the rate is the average of a 1-or-0 flag that is 1 for a cancelled ride. Round that average to 2 places."],"approach":"Filter both sides of the join, then aggregate. The rider and the driver are two different rows in `members`, so the table is joined twice with a different alias each time.\n\nThe rate is `AVG(cancelled_flag)`. Writing the flag as 1.0 or 0.0 avoids whole-number division.\n\nA banned person on **either** side removes the ride, and the date window is part of the question.\n\n```sql\nSELECT r.ride_date,\n       ROUND(AVG(CASE WHEN r.status = 'completed' THEN 0.0 ELSE 1.0 END), 2) AS cancellation_rate\nFROM rides r\nJOIN members rider  ON rider.member_id  = r.rider_id  AND rider.banned  = 'No'\nJOIN members driver ON driver.member_id = r.driver_id AND driver.banned = 'No'\nWHERE r.ride_date BETWEEN DATE '2013-10-01' AND DATE '2013-10-03'\nGROUP BY r.ride_date\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["members(member_id INT, banned VARCHAR, role VARCHAR)   -- banned is 'Yes' or 'No'","rides(ride_id INT, rider_id INT, driver_id INT, status VARCHAR, ride_date DATE)   -- status: 'completed', 'cancelled_by_driver', 'cancelled_by_rider'"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE members(member_id INT, banned VARCHAR, role VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO members VALUES (1, 'No', 'rider'), (2, 'Yes', 'rider'), (3, 'No', 'rider'), (4, 'No', 'rider'), (10, 'No', 'driver'), (11, 'No', 'driver'), (12, 'No', 'driver'), (13, 'No', 'driver')","CREATE TABLE rides(ride_id INT, rider_id INT, driver_id INT, status VARCHAR, ride_date DATE)","INSERT INTO rides VALUES (1, 1, 10, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-01'), (2, 2, 11, 'cancelled_by_driver', DATE '2013-10-01'), (3, 3, 12, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-01'), (4, 4, 13, 'cancelled_by_driver', DATE '2013-10-01'), (5, 1, 10, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-02'), (6, 2, 11, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-02'), (7, 3, 12, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-02'), (8, 2, 12, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-03'), (9, 3, 10, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-03'), (10, 4, 13, 'cancelled_by_driver', DATE '2013-10-03')"],"tables":[{"name":"members","cols":"member_id INT, banned VARCHAR, role VARCHAR","rows":[[1,"No","rider"],[2,"Yes","rider"],[3,"No","rider"],[4,"No","rider"],[10,"No","driver"],[11,"No","driver"],[12,"No","driver"],[13,"No","driver"]]},{"name":"rides","cols":"ride_id INT, rider_id INT, driver_id INT, status VARCHAR, ride_date DATE","rows":[[1,1,10,"completed",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-01'"}],[2,2,11,"cancelled_by_driver",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-01'"}],[3,3,12,"completed",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-01'"}],[4,4,13,"cancelled_by_driver",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-01'"}],[5,1,10,"completed",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-02'"}],[6,2,11,"completed",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-02'"}],[7,3,12,"completed",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-02'"}],[8,2,12,"completed",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-03'"}],[9,3,10,"completed",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-03'"}],[10,4,13,"cancelled_by_driver",{"sql":"DATE '2013-10-03'"}]]}],"expected":{"columns":["ride_date","cancellation_rate"],"rows":[["2013-10-01",0.33],["2013-10-02",0],["2013-10-03",0.5]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE members(member_id INT, banned VARCHAR, role VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO members VALUES (1, 'No', 'rider'), (5, 'Yes', 'driver'), (6, 'No', 'driver')","CREATE TABLE rides(ride_id INT, rider_id INT, driver_id INT, status VARCHAR, ride_date DATE)","INSERT INTO rides VALUES (1, 1, 5, 'cancelled_by_rider', DATE '2013-10-01'), (2, 1, 6, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-01')"],"expected":{"columns":["ride_date","cancellation_rate"],"rows":[["2013-10-01",0]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE members(member_id INT, banned VARCHAR, role VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO members VALUES (1, 'No', 'rider'), (2, 'No', 'driver')","CREATE TABLE rides(ride_id INT, rider_id INT, driver_id INT, status VARCHAR, ride_date DATE)","INSERT INTO rides VALUES (1, 1, 2, 'completed', DATE '2013-09-30'), (2, 1, 2, 'cancelled_by_rider', DATE '2013-10-02'), (3, 1, 2, 'cancelled_by_driver', DATE '2013-10-02'), (4, 1, 2, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-04')"],"expected":{"columns":["ride_date","cancellation_rate"],"rows":[["2013-10-02",1]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE members(member_id INT, banned VARCHAR, role VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO members VALUES (1, 'Yes', 'rider'), (2, 'No', 'driver'), (3, 'No', 'rider')","CREATE TABLE rides(ride_id INT, rider_id INT, driver_id INT, status VARCHAR, ride_date DATE)","INSERT INTO rides VALUES (1, 1, 2, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-01'), (2, 3, 2, 'completed', DATE '2013-10-03')"],"expected":{"columns":["ride_date","cancellation_rate"],"rows":[["2013-10-03",0]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d11-next-day-retention","day":11,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 550","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Game Play Analysis IV","statement":"Each row says a player logged in on a device on a date. A player's **first login** is their earliest `event_date`.\n\nReturn the **fraction of players** who logged in again on the **day right after** their first login, rounded to 2 decimals, in a column named `fraction`.\n\nA player counts once, even if they logged in twice on that next day from two devices.","starter":"-- Return: fraction\nSELECT\n","hints":["Split it in two: first find each player's first login date, then check whether that player has a row exactly one day later.","Join the first-login result back to plays on player_id and event_date = first date + 1.","Divide the number of distinct players found by the number of distinct players overall."],"approach":"The numerator and denominator count **players**, not rows. A player can have several rows on the day after their first login, so the numerator uses `COUNT(DISTINCT player_id)`.\n\nCompute each player's first date once in a CTE. Reuse it for the join and for the denominator. `date + 1` moves a date forward by one day, and it handles month and year ends for you.\n\n```sql\nWITH first_login AS (\n  SELECT player_id, MIN(event_date) AS d FROM plays GROUP BY player_id\n)\nSELECT ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT p.player_id) * 1.0 / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM first_login), 2) AS fraction\nFROM first_login f\nJOIN plays p ON p.player_id = f.player_id AND p.event_date = f.d + 1\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["plays(player_id INT, device_id INT, event_date DATE, games INT)   -- one row per player, device and day"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE plays(player_id INT, device_id INT, event_date DATE, games INT)","INSERT INTO plays VALUES (1, 2, DATE '2016-03-01', 5), (1, 2, DATE '2016-03-02', 6), (2, 3, DATE '2017-06-25', 1), (3, 1, DATE '2016-03-02', 0), (3, 4, DATE '2018-07-03', 5)"],"tables":[{"name":"plays","cols":"player_id INT, device_id INT, event_date DATE, games INT","rows":[[1,2,{"sql":"DATE '2016-03-01'"},5],[1,2,{"sql":"DATE '2016-03-02'"},6],[2,3,{"sql":"DATE '2017-06-25'"},1],[3,1,{"sql":"DATE '2016-03-02'"},0],[3,4,{"sql":"DATE '2018-07-03'"},5]]}],"expected":{"columns":["fraction"],"rows":[[0.33]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE plays(player_id INT, device_id INT, event_date DATE, games INT)","INSERT INTO plays VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2020-01-01', 1), (1, 2, DATE '2020-01-02', 1), (1, 3, DATE '2020-01-02', 1)"],"expected":{"columns":["fraction"],"rows":[[1]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE plays(player_id INT, device_id INT, event_date DATE, games INT)","INSERT INTO plays VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2020-01-01', 1), (1, 1, DATE '2020-01-03', 1), (2, 1, DATE '2020-01-05', 1)"],"expected":{"columns":["fraction"],"rows":[[0]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE plays(player_id INT, device_id INT, event_date DATE, games INT)","INSERT INTO plays VALUES (1, 1, DATE '2020-02-28', 1), (1, 1, DATE '2020-02-29', 1), (2, 1, DATE '2020-12-31', 1), (2, 1, DATE '2021-01-01', 1)"],"expected":{"columns":["fraction"],"rows":[[1]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d12-monthly-transactions","day":12,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 1193","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Monthly Transactions I","statement":"For each **month** and **country**, report:\n\n- `trans_count`: how many transactions there were\n- `approved_count`: how many were approved\n- `trans_total_amount`: the sum of all their amounts\n- `approved_total_amount`: the sum of the approved amounts\n\nShow the month as text in the form `YYYY-MM` in a column named `month`. When a group has no approved transactions, the approved columns are **0**, not NULL.","starter":"-- Return: month, country, trans_count, approved_count, trans_total_amount, approved_total_amount\nSELECT\n","hints":["The group is a (month, country) pair. Turn the date into a text month first, then GROUP BY it with country.","For \"approved only\" numbers, put a CASE inside the aggregate: SUM(CASE WHEN state = 'approved' THEN amount ELSE 0 END).","Use ELSE 0 so a group with nothing approved gives 0. Without an ELSE the CASE gives NULL and SUM of only NULLs is NULL."],"approach":"This is **conditional aggregation**: one pass over the table with a `CASE` inside each aggregate, instead of several queries joined together.\n\n`strftime(trans_date, '%Y-%m')` makes the month key. It keeps the year, so January 2019 and January 2020 stay apart.\n\n`SUM` of a CASE with no ELSE returns NULL when no row matched. `ELSE 0` is what turns that into the 0 the question asks for.\n\n```sql\nSELECT strftime(trans_date, '%Y-%m') AS month, country,\n       COUNT(*) AS trans_count,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN state = 'approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS approved_count,\n       SUM(amount) AS trans_total_amount,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN state = 'approved' THEN amount ELSE 0 END) AS approved_total_amount\nFROM transactions\nGROUP BY 1, 2\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["transactions(id INT, country VARCHAR, state VARCHAR, amount INT, trans_date DATE)   -- state: 'approved' or 'declined'"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE transactions(id INT, country VARCHAR, state VARCHAR, amount INT, trans_date DATE)","INSERT INTO transactions VALUES (121, 'US', 'approved', 1000, DATE '2018-12-18'), (122, 'US', 'declined', 2000, DATE '2018-12-19'), (123, 'US', 'approved', 2000, DATE '2019-01-01'), (124, 'DE', 'approved', 2000, DATE '2019-01-07')"],"tables":[{"name":"transactions","cols":"id INT, country VARCHAR, state VARCHAR, amount INT, trans_date DATE","rows":[[121,"US","approved",1000,{"sql":"DATE '2018-12-18'"}],[122,"US","declined",2000,{"sql":"DATE '2018-12-19'"}],[123,"US","approved",2000,{"sql":"DATE '2019-01-01'"}],[124,"DE","approved",2000,{"sql":"DATE '2019-01-07'"}]]}],"expected":{"columns":["month","country","trans_count","approved_count","trans_total_amount","approved_total_amount"],"rows":[["2018-12","US",2,1,3000,1000],["2019-01","US",1,1,2000,2000],["2019-01","DE",1,1,2000,2000]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE transactions(id INT, country VARCHAR, state VARCHAR, amount INT, trans_date DATE)","INSERT INTO transactions VALUES (1, 'FR', 'declined', 500, DATE '2020-03-01'), (2, 'FR', 'declined', 700, DATE '2020-03-15')"],"expected":{"columns":["month","country","trans_count","approved_count","trans_total_amount","approved_total_amount"],"rows":[["2020-03","FR",2,0,1200,0]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE transactions(id INT, country VARCHAR, state VARCHAR, amount INT, trans_date DATE)","INSERT INTO transactions VALUES (1, 'US', 'approved', 10, DATE '2019-12-31'), (2, 'US', 'approved', 20, DATE '2020-12-01'), (3, 'US', 'declined', 5, DATE '2020-12-02')"],"expected":{"columns":["month","country","trans_count","approved_count","trans_total_amount","approved_total_amount"],"rows":[["2019-12","US",1,1,10,10],["2020-12","US",2,1,25,20]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE transactions(id INT, country VARCHAR, state VARCHAR, amount INT, trans_date DATE)","INSERT INTO transactions VALUES (1, 'US', 'approved', 100, DATE '2021-05-01'), (2, 'CA', 'approved', 50, DATE '2021-05-02'), (3, 'CA', 'declined', 25, DATE '2021-05-03')"],"expected":{"columns":["month","country","trans_count","approved_count","trans_total_amount","approved_total_amount"],"rows":[["2021-05","US",1,1,100,100],["2021-05","CA",2,1,75,50]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"sql-d13-movie-rating","day":13,"lang":"sql","anchor":"LC 1341","cat":"PRACTICE","bounty":20,"title":"Movie Rating","statement":"Return **two rows** in one column named `results`:\n\n1. The **name of the user** who rated the most movies. If users tie, return the name that comes first alphabetically.\n2. The **title of the movie** with the highest **average rating in February 2020**. If movies tie, return the title that comes first alphabetically.\n\nThe two rows can come back in any order.","starter":"-- Return one column: results (two rows)\nSELECT\n","hints":["There are two separate questions. Write each as its own query that returns one row, then stack them.","Each query is GROUP BY, ORDER BY the measure descending then the name or title ascending, and LIMIT 1. The tie-break is the second ORDER BY column.","Wrap each query in parentheses and join them with UNION ALL. Filter the movie query to 2020-02-01 up to but not including 2020-03-01."],"approach":"Each half is a \"top 1 with a tie-break\" query: `ORDER BY measure DESC, name ASC LIMIT 1`. The second `ORDER BY` column is the tie rule, so a tie can never leave the answer to chance.\n\nParentheses around each half let each have its own `ORDER BY` and `LIMIT`, and `UNION ALL` stacks the two single-row answers.\n\nUse a half-open date range (`>= Feb 1` and `\u003c Mar 1`). It includes 29 February and never breaks on the last day of the month.\n\n```sql\n(SELECT u.name AS results\n FROM ratings r JOIN users u ON u.user_id = r.user_id\n GROUP BY u.user_id, u.name\n ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, u.name\n LIMIT 1)\nUNION ALL\n(SELECT m.title\n FROM ratings r JOIN movies m ON m.movie_id = r.movie_id\n WHERE r.created_at >= DATE '2020-02-01' AND r.created_at \u003c DATE '2020-03-01'\n GROUP BY m.movie_id, m.title\n ORDER BY AVG(r.rating) DESC, m.title\n LIMIT 1)\n```","kind":"select","ordered":false,"schema":["users(user_id INT, name VARCHAR)","movies(movie_id INT, title VARCHAR)","ratings(movie_id INT, user_id INT, rating INT, created_at DATE)"],"cases":[{"setup":["CREATE TABLE users(user_id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO users VALUES (1, 'Daniel'), (2, 'Monica'), (3, 'Maria'), (4, 'James')","CREATE TABLE movies(movie_id INT, title VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO movies VALUES (1, 'Avengers'), (2, 'Frozen 2'), (3, 'Joker')","CREATE TABLE ratings(movie_id INT, user_id INT, rating INT, created_at DATE)","INSERT INTO ratings VALUES (1, 1, 3, DATE '2020-01-12'), (1, 2, 4, DATE '2020-02-11'), (1, 3, 2, DATE '2020-02-12'), (1, 4, 1, DATE '2020-01-01'), (2, 1, 5, DATE '2020-02-17'), (2, 2, 2, DATE '2020-02-01'), (2, 3, 2, DATE '2020-03-01'), (3, 1, 3, DATE '2020-02-22'), (3, 2, 4, DATE '2020-02-25')"],"tables":[{"name":"users","cols":"user_id INT, name VARCHAR","rows":[[1,"Daniel"],[2,"Monica"],[3,"Maria"],[4,"James"]]},{"name":"movies","cols":"movie_id INT, title VARCHAR","rows":[[1,"Avengers"],[2,"Frozen 2"],[3,"Joker"]]},{"name":"ratings","cols":"movie_id INT, user_id INT, rating INT, created_at DATE","rows":[[1,1,3,{"sql":"DATE '2020-01-12'"}],[1,2,4,{"sql":"DATE '2020-02-11'"}],[1,3,2,{"sql":"DATE '2020-02-12'"}],[1,4,1,{"sql":"DATE '2020-01-01'"}],[2,1,5,{"sql":"DATE '2020-02-17'"}],[2,2,2,{"sql":"DATE '2020-02-01'"}],[2,3,2,{"sql":"DATE '2020-03-01'"}],[3,1,3,{"sql":"DATE '2020-02-22'"}],[3,2,4,{"sql":"DATE '2020-02-25'"}]]}],"expected":{"columns":["results"],"rows":[["Daniel"],["Frozen 2"]]}},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE users(user_id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO users VALUES (1, 'Zed'), (2, 'Amy')","CREATE TABLE movies(movie_id INT, title VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO movies VALUES (1, 'Alpha'), (2, 'Beta')","CREATE TABLE ratings(movie_id INT, user_id INT, rating INT, created_at DATE)","INSERT INTO ratings VALUES (1, 1, 4, DATE '2020-02-03'), (2, 1, 4, DATE '2020-02-04'), (1, 2, 4, DATE '2020-02-05')"],"expected":{"columns":["results"],"rows":[["Zed"],["Alpha"]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE users(user_id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO users VALUES (1, 'Zed'), (2, 'Amy')","CREATE TABLE movies(movie_id INT, title VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO movies VALUES (1, 'Gamma'), (2, 'Delta')","CREATE TABLE ratings(movie_id INT, user_id INT, rating INT, created_at DATE)","INSERT INTO ratings VALUES (1, 1, 3, DATE '2020-02-03'), (2, 2, 3, DATE '2020-02-04')"],"expected":{"columns":["results"],"rows":[["Amy"],["Delta"]]},"hidden":true},{"setup":["CREATE TABLE users(user_id INT, name VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO users VALUES (1, 'Kim')","CREATE TABLE movies(movie_id INT, title VARCHAR)","INSERT INTO movies VALUES (1, 'Old'), (2, 'Leap')","CREATE TABLE ratings(movie_id INT, user_id INT, rating INT, created_at DATE)","INSERT INTO ratings VALUES (1, 1, 5, DATE '2020-01-31'), (1, 1, 5, DATE '2020-03-01'), (2, 1, 3, DATE '2020-02-29'), (1, 1, 1, DATE '2020-02-02')"],"expected":{"columns":["results"],"rows":[["Kim"],["Leap"]]},"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d15-group-anagrams","day":15,"lang":"python","anchor":"Group Anagrams","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Group Anagrams","statement":"Given a list of lowercase words, group the words that are **anagrams** of each other: the same letters in a different order.\n\nReturn a list of groups. The order of the groups, and the order of the words inside a group, does not matter. A word that appears twice appears twice in its group.","starter":"def group_anagrams(strs):\n    # Return a list of groups of words that are anagrams of each other.\n    pass\n","hints":["You need one value that is the same for every word in a group and different between groups. What stays the same when you shuffle the letters of a word?","Sort the letters of the word. \"eat\", \"tea\" and \"ate\" all become \"aet\". A dict from that key to a list of words collects each group.","A list cannot be a dict key because it can change. Turn the sorted letters into a string or a tuple first."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"group things that are equal under some rule\". **Pattern:** compute a canonical key for each item and use a hashmap from key to list. **Complexity:** O(n · k log k) time for n words of length k, O(n · k) space.\n\nThe key must make two words collide **only** when they are anagrams. Sorted letters do. The sum of letter codes does not: \"ad\" and \"bc\" have the same sum.\n\nWhen every word is a lowercase letter string, a 26-count tuple is a key too, and it makes the time O(n · k).\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef group_anagrams(strs):\n    groups = defaultdict(list)\n    for word in strs:\n        groups[tuple(sorted(word))].append(word)   # same letters -> same key\n    return list(groups.values())\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"groups","fn":"group_anagrams","signature":"def group_anagrams(strs: list[str]) -> list[list[str]]","cases":[{"args":[["eat","tea","tan","ate","nat","bat"]],"expected":[["eat","tea","ate"],["tan","nat"],["bat"]]},{"args":[[""]],"expected":[[""]]},{"args":[["a"]],"expected":[["a"]],"hidden":true},{"args":[["ab","ba","ab"]],"expected":[["ab","ba","ab"]],"hidden":true},{"args":[["ad","bc"]],"expected":[["ad"],["bc"]],"hidden":true},{"args":[["abc","bca","cab","xyz","zyx","aab","aba"]],"expected":[["abc","bca","cab"],["xyz","zyx"],["aab","aba"]],"hidden":true},{"args":[[]],"expected":[],"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d15-top-k-frequent","day":15,"lang":"python","anchor":"Top K Frequent Elements","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Top K Frequent Elements","statement":"Given a list of integers `nums` and an integer `k`, return the `k` values that appear **most often**. Return them in any order.\n\nThe answer is unique: there is never a tie for the last place in the top `k`.","starter":"def top_k_frequent(nums, k):\n    # Return the k most frequent values.\n    pass\n","hints":["Two steps: count how often each value appears, then pick the k biggest counts.","collections.Counter does the counting. For the picking, you can sort the counts, use a heap of size k, or bucket the values by their count.","Counter.most_common(k) returns the k pairs with the biggest counts. Take the value from each pair."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"top k by frequency\". **Pattern:** a counter, then a selection step. **Complexity:** sorting the counts is O(n log n); a min-heap of size k is O(n log k); bucket sort by count is O(n).\n\nGive both complexities when you narrate. Bucket sort works because a count can never be more than `len(nums)`: make a list of that size, put each value in the slot for its count, and read from the high end.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef top_k_frequent(nums, k):\n    counts = Counter(nums)\n    return [value for value, _ in counts.most_common(k)]\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"unordered","fn":"top_k_frequent","signature":"def top_k_frequent(nums: list[int], k: int) -> list[int]","cases":[{"args":[[1,1,1,2,2,3],2],"expected":[1,2]},{"args":[[1],1],"expected":[1]},{"args":[[4,4,4,4,5,5,5,6,6,7],3],"expected":[4,5,6],"hidden":true},{"args":[[-1,-1,-2,-2,-2,3],1],"expected":[-2],"hidden":true},{"args":[[5,3,3,5,5,2,2,2,2,9],2],"expected":[2,5],"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,2],2],"expected":[1,2],"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d15-two-sum","day":15,"lang":"python","anchor":"Two Sum","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Two Sum","statement":"Given a list of integers `nums` and an integer `target`, return the **indices** of the two numbers that add up to `target`.\n\nExactly one pair works. You may not use the same element twice. Return the two indices in any order.","starter":"def two_sum(nums, target):\n    # Return the indices of the two numbers that add up to target.\n    pass\n","hints":["The slow way checks every pair. Ask what you would need to know about the earlier numbers to answer \"is there a partner for this one?\" in one step.","For the number x you are looking at, its partner is target - x. A dict can tell you in O(1) whether you have already seen that value, and where.","Walk the list once. For each x, look for target - x in the dict first. If it is there, you are done. If not, store x with its index and move on."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"find a pair that adds up to something\" and you only need a yes, a count or an index, not sorted order. **Pattern:** one pass with a hashmap that remembers what you have seen. **Complexity:** O(n) time, O(n) space.\n\nThe order inside the loop is the whole trick. **Look up first, insert second.** If you insert first, a number can match itself: with `[3, 2, 4]` and target 6, the first 3 would pair with the 3 you just stored.\n\n```python\ndef two_sum(nums, target):\n    seen = {}                      # value -> index\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        if target - x in seen:     # check before you insert\n            return [seen[target - x], i]\n        seen[x] = i\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"unordered","fn":"two_sum","signature":"def two_sum(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]","cases":[{"args":[[2,7,11,15],9],"expected":[0,1]},{"args":[[3,2,4],6],"expected":[1,2]},{"args":[[3,3],6],"expected":[0,1],"hidden":true},{"args":[[-1,-2,-3,-4,-5],-8],"expected":[2,4],"hidden":true},{"args":[[0,4,3,0],0],"expected":[0,3],"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,5,9,14,20],23],"expected":[2,3],"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d16-container-with-most-water","day":16,"lang":"python","anchor":"Container With Most Water","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Container With Most Water","statement":"You have vertical lines at positions 0, 1, 2, ... and `height[i]` is the height of line `i`. Pick two lines. Together with the x-axis they form a container.\n\nThe water it holds is the **distance between the lines** times the **shorter** of the two heights. Return the largest amount of water any pair can hold.","starter":"def max_area(height):\n    # Return the most water any two lines can hold.\n    pass\n","hints":["Start with the widest container: the first and last lines. Any narrower container needs to be taller to beat it.","The shorter line decides the height. Moving the taller line inward can only make the width smaller, so it can never help.","Compute the area, keep the best, then move the pointer at the shorter line inward. Stop when the pointers meet."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"best pair in a sequence\" where the value depends on both ends. **Pattern:** converging two pointers that always discard the end that cannot improve the answer. **Complexity:** O(n) time, O(1) space.\n\nSay why moving the shorter side is safe: with the shorter side fixed, every container that keeps it is narrower and no taller, so none beats what you already recorded.\n\nThe brute-force double loop is O(n²). Mention it, then improve it.\n\n```python\ndef max_area(height):\n    i, j = 0, len(height) - 1\n    best = 0\n    while i \u003c j:\n        best = max(best, (j - i) * min(height[i], height[j]))\n        if height[i] \u003c height[j]:      # the shorter line limits the area, so move it\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            j -= 1\n    return best\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"max_area","signature":"def max_area(height: list[int]) -> int","cases":[{"args":[[1,8,6,2,5,4,8,3,7]],"expected":49},{"args":[[1,1]],"expected":1},{"args":[[4,3,2,1,4]],"expected":16,"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,2,1]],"expected":2,"hidden":true},{"args":[[2,3,4,5,18,17,6]],"expected":17,"hidden":true},{"args":[[9,8,7,6]],"expected":18,"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d16-longest-substring","day":16,"lang":"python","anchor":"Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","statement":"Given a string `s`, return the length of the longest **substring** (a run of neighbouring characters) in which no character appears twice.\n\nAn empty string has an answer of 0.","starter":"def length_of_longest_substring(s):\n    # Return the length of the longest substring with no repeated character.\n    pass\n","hints":["Keep a window [left, right] that never holds a repeated character. Grow right one step at a time and record the window size.","When the new character is already inside the window, move left to just past its earlier copy. A dict from character to its last index tells you where that copy is.","Only move left forward, never back: check that the earlier copy is at or after left before you jump."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"longest contiguous stretch with a property\". **Pattern:** sliding window. **Invariant:** the window `s[left..right]` has no repeated character. **Complexity:** O(n) time, O(min(n, alphabet)) space.\n\nThe classic bug is on \"abba\". When the second `a` arrives, its earlier copy is at index 0, which is **left of** the window start. If you jump left back to 1, the window \"bba\" holds a repeat. Guard the jump with `last[c] >= left`.\n\n```python\ndef length_of_longest_substring(s):\n    last = {}                  # char -> index where it was last seen\n    left = best = 0\n    for right, c in enumerate(s):\n        if c in last and last[c] >= left:\n            left = last[c] + 1  # jump past the earlier copy\n        last[c] = right\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"length_of_longest_substring","signature":"def length_of_longest_substring(s: str) -> int","cases":[{"args":["abcabcbb"],"expected":3},{"args":["bbbbb"],"expected":1},{"args":["pwwkew"],"expected":3},{"args":[""],"expected":0,"hidden":true},{"args":["abba"],"expected":2,"hidden":true},{"args":["dvdf"],"expected":3,"hidden":true},{"args":["tmmzuxt"],"expected":5,"hidden":true},{"args":["abcdef"],"expected":6,"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d16-valid-palindrome","day":16,"lang":"python","anchor":"Valid Palindrome","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Valid Palindrome","statement":"A phrase is a palindrome if it reads the same forwards and backwards after you **lowercase it and remove everything that is not a letter or a digit**.\n\nReturn `True` if `s` is a palindrome and `False` if not. An empty result, such as a string of spaces, counts as a palindrome.","starter":"def is_palindrome(s):\n    # Ignore case and anything that is not a letter or a digit.\n    pass\n","hints":["Two pointers fit a mirror check: one from the left end, one from the right end, moving toward the middle.","Before comparing, move each pointer past characters that are not letters or digits. str.isalnum() tells you which are.","Compare the two characters in lowercase. The first mismatch means False. If the pointers meet, it is a palindrome."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"reads the same from both ends\". **Pattern:** converging two pointers. **Complexity:** O(n) time and O(1) extra space, because you never build a cleaned copy.\n\nBuilding a cleaned string and comparing it with its reverse is simpler and also correct, at the cost of O(n) space. Name the trade-off.\n\nTwo traps: `isalpha()` drops digits, so \"0P\" would look like a palindrome, and an all-punctuation string is a palindrome by the rules above.\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(s):\n    i, j = 0, len(s) - 1\n    while i \u003c j:\n        while i \u003c j and not s[i].isalnum():\n            i += 1\n        while i \u003c j and not s[j].isalnum():\n            j -= 1\n        if s[i].lower() != s[j].lower():\n            return False\n        i += 1\n        j -= 1\n    return True\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"is_palindrome","signature":"def is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool","cases":[{"args":["A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"],"expected":true},{"args":["race a car"],"expected":false},{"args":[" "],"expected":true,"hidden":true},{"args":["0P"],"expected":false,"hidden":true},{"args":[".,"],"expected":true,"hidden":true},{"args":["ab_a"],"expected":true,"hidden":true},{"args":["Was it a car or a cat I saw?"],"expected":true,"hidden":true},{"args":["abca"],"expected":false,"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d17-daily-temperatures","day":17,"lang":"python","anchor":"Daily Temperatures","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Daily Temperatures","statement":"Given a list of daily temperatures, return a list of the same length. For each day, the answer is **how many days you wait** until a **strictly warmer** day.\n\nIf no warmer day ever comes, the answer for that day is 0.","starter":"def daily_temperatures(temperatures):\n    # For each day, how many days until a strictly warmer one? 0 if never.\n    pass\n","hints":["The slow way scans forward from every day. Think about which days are still waiting for an answer as you walk left to right.","Keep a stack of the indices of days that have not found a warmer day yet. Their temperatures are in non-increasing order from the bottom to the top.","When today is warmer than the day on top of the stack, pop it and its answer is today's index minus its index. Repeat while that stays true, then push today."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"next greater element to the right\". **Pattern:** a monotonic stack of **indices**. **Complexity:** O(n) time, because each index is pushed once and popped once, and O(n) space.\n\nPush indices, not temperatures: you need the index to compute the distance. The comparison is strict, so an equal temperature does not pop anything.\n\n```python\ndef daily_temperatures(temperatures):\n    answer = [0] * len(temperatures)\n    stack = []                            # indices of days still waiting for a warmer day\n    for i, t in enumerate(temperatures):\n        while stack and temperatures[stack[-1]] \u003c t:\n            j = stack.pop()\n            answer[j] = i - j\n        stack.append(i)\n    return answer\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"daily_temperatures","signature":"def daily_temperatures(temperatures: list[int]) -> list[int]","cases":[{"args":[[73,74,75,71,69,72,76,73]],"expected":[1,1,4,2,1,1,0,0]},{"args":[[30,40,50,60]],"expected":[1,1,1,0]},{"args":[[30,60,90]],"expected":[1,1,0],"hidden":true},{"args":[[90,80,70]],"expected":[0,0,0],"hidden":true},{"args":[[50]],"expected":[0],"hidden":true},{"args":[[70,70,70,71]],"expected":[3,2,1,0],"hidden":true},{"args":[[]],"expected":[],"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d17-min-stack","day":17,"lang":"python","anchor":"Min Stack","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Min Stack","statement":"Design a stack that supports `push(val)`, `pop()`, `top()` and `getMin()`. All four must run in **O(1)** time.\n\n`getMin()` returns the smallest value currently in the stack. `pop()` and `top()` are only called on a non-empty stack.\n\nName your class `MinStack`. The checker builds one, calls the methods in order, and compares what each call returns. Methods that return nothing are expected to return `None`.","starter":"class MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        pass\n\n    def push(self, val):\n        pass\n\n    def pop(self):\n        pass\n\n    def top(self):\n        pass\n\n    def getMin(self):\n        pass\n","hints":["min(stack) works but takes O(n). You need the minimum to be ready before anyone asks for it.","When you push a value, you already know the smallest value in the stack at that moment. Store that number next to the value.","Keep pairs (value, smallest so far). Pop removes the pair, so the previous smallest is automatically back on top."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"a stack with an extra O(1) query\". **Pattern:** store the answer to the query alongside each element. **Complexity:** O(1) per call, O(n) space.\n\nThe minimum **so far** at each depth never changes after the push, and popping only removes the newest depth. That is why a single running minimum fails: after `pop` it still holds a value that is gone.\n\nA second stack of minimums also works. If you push to it only on strictly smaller values, equal values break it, so push on `\u003c=`.\n\n```python\nclass MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []        # each entry is (value, smallest value at or below it)\n\n    def push(self, val):\n        low = min(val, self.stack[-1][1]) if self.stack else val\n        self.stack.append((val, low))\n\n    def pop(self):\n        self.stack.pop()\n\n    def top(self):\n        return self.stack[-1][0]\n\n    def getMin(self):\n        return self.stack[-1][1]\n```","kind":"class","cmp":"eq","cls":"MinStack","signature":"class MinStack: push(val), pop(), top(), getMin()","cases":[{"args":[[],[-2],[0],[-3],[],[],[],[]],"ops":["MinStack","push","push","push","getMin","pop","top","getMin"],"expected":[null,null,null,null,-3,null,0,-2]},{"args":[[],[7],[],[]],"ops":["MinStack","push","top","getMin"],"expected":[null,null,7,7]},{"args":[[],[2],[2],[1],[],[],[],[],[]],"ops":["MinStack","push","push","push","getMin","pop","getMin","pop","getMin"],"expected":[null,null,null,null,1,null,2,null,2],"hidden":true},{"args":[[],[5],[3],[],[],[],[4],[]],"ops":["MinStack","push","push","getMin","pop","getMin","push","getMin"],"expected":[null,null,null,3,null,5,null,4],"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d17-valid-parentheses","day":17,"lang":"python","anchor":"Valid Parentheses","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Valid Parentheses","statement":"A string contains only the characters `(`, `)`, `[`, `]`, `{` and `}`. It is **valid** when every opening bracket is closed by the same type of bracket, in the right order.\n\nReturn `True` if `s` is valid. An empty string is valid.","starter":"def is_valid(s):\n    # Return True if every bracket is closed by the right type, in the right order.\n    pass\n","hints":["The most recent opening bracket must be the first one to close. Which data structure gives you \"last in, first out\"?","Push each opening bracket. When you see a closing bracket, pop and check that it is the matching type.","Two ways to fail: a closing bracket with nothing to pop, and opening brackets still on the stack at the end."],"approach":"**Trigger:** nesting or \"match the most recent open thing\". **Pattern:** a stack. **Complexity:** O(n) time, O(n) space.\n\nA dict from each closing bracket to its opening partner keeps the check short. Test three edge cases before you submit: an empty string, a lone `)` (pop on an empty stack), and a lone `(` (leftover on the stack).\n\n```python\ndef is_valid(s):\n    pairs = {\")\": \"(\", \"]\": \"[\", \"}\": \"{\"}\n    stack = []\n    for c in s:\n        if c in pairs:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[c]:\n                return False\n        else:\n            stack.append(c)\n    return not stack\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"is_valid","signature":"def is_valid(s: str) -> bool","cases":[{"args":["()"],"expected":true},{"args":["()[]{}"],"expected":true},{"args":["(]"],"expected":false},{"args":["([)]"],"expected":false,"hidden":true},{"args":["{[]}"],"expected":true,"hidden":true},{"args":["("],"expected":false,"hidden":true},{"args":[")"],"expected":false,"hidden":true},{"args":[""],"expected":true,"hidden":true},{"args":["((()))"],"expected":true,"hidden":true},{"args":["}{"],"expected":false,"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d18-binary-search","day":18,"lang":"python","anchor":"Binary Search","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Binary Search","statement":"`nums` is a list of **distinct integers sorted from smallest to largest**. Return the index of `target` in `nums`, or `-1` if it is not there.\n\nYour solution should take O(log n) time.","starter":"def search(nums, target):\n    # Return the index of target, or -1.\n    pass\n","hints":["Because the list is sorted, one comparison with the middle element lets you throw away half of what is left.","Keep two indices, lo and hi, for the part that could still hold the target. Compare with nums[mid]: equal means found, smaller means search right, larger means search left.","When you move, skip mid itself (mid + 1 or mid - 1). Loop while lo \u003c= hi, so a range with one element still gets checked."],"approach":"**Trigger:** a sorted sequence and a \"find or locate\" question. **Pattern:** binary search that keeps an invariant: if the target exists, it is inside `[lo, hi]`. **Complexity:** O(log n) time, O(1) space.\n\nThe loop condition is where bugs live. With `hi = len(nums) - 1` (inclusive), use `lo \u003c= hi`. With `lo \u003c hi` a one-element list is never examined.\n\n```python\ndef search(nums, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while lo \u003c= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        if nums[mid] \u003c target:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid - 1\n    return -1\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"search","signature":"def search(nums: list[int], target: int) -> int","cases":[{"args":[[-1,0,3,5,9,12],9],"expected":4},{"args":[[-1,0,3,5,9,12],2],"expected":-1},{"args":[[5],5],"expected":0,"hidden":true},{"args":[[5],-5],"expected":-1,"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,3,5,7,9],1],"expected":0,"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,3,5,7,9],9],"expected":4,"hidden":true},{"args":[[2,4],4],"expected":1,"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,2,3,4],0],"expected":-1,"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d18-koko-eating-bananas","day":18,"lang":"python","anchor":"Koko Eating Bananas","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Koko Eating Bananas","statement":"Koko has piles of bananas and `h` hours. She picks a speed of `k` bananas per hour. Each hour she chooses one pile and eats up to `k` bananas from it. If the pile has fewer than `k`, she finishes it and **does not start another pile that hour**.\n\nReturn the **smallest whole number `k`** that lets her eat every pile within `h` hours. There are never fewer hours than piles.","starter":"def min_eating_speed(piles, h):\n    # Return the smallest speed k that finishes every pile within h hours.\n    pass\n","hints":["Do not search for the pile. Search for the **speed**: if speed k is fast enough, then every speed above k is too.","For a given k, the hours needed are the sum over piles of ceil(pile / k). The speed is somewhere between 1 and the biggest pile.","Binary search the speed. If k finishes in h hours or fewer, try a slower speed (hi = k). Otherwise go faster (lo = k + 1). Stop when lo meets hi."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"find the minimum value that makes a yes/no check pass\", where the check is monotonic. **Pattern:** binary search on the **answer**. **Complexity:** O(n log m) time for n piles and a biggest pile of m, O(1) space.\n\nEach check is a full pass over the piles. The search space is the speeds 1 to `max(piles)`, which halves each step. Counting up from 1 would take `max(piles)` steps, which is far too slow for a pile of a billion.\n\nRound the hours **up**: a pile of 7 at speed 3 takes 3 hours, not 2. In Python, `-(-p // k)` is ceiling division.\n\n```python\ndef min_eating_speed(piles, h):\n    lo, hi = 1, max(piles)                 # the answer is somewhere in [1, max pile]\n    while lo \u003c hi:\n        k = (lo + hi) // 2\n        hours = sum(-(-p // k) for p in piles)   # ceiling division\n        if hours \u003c= h:\n            hi = k                          # k works, maybe a smaller one does too\n        else:\n            lo = k + 1                      # too slow\n    return lo\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"min_eating_speed","signature":"def min_eating_speed(piles: list[int], h: int) -> int","cases":[{"args":[[3,6,7,11],8],"expected":4},{"args":[[30,11,23,4,20],5],"expected":30},{"args":[[30,11,23,4,20],6],"expected":23},{"args":[[1],1],"expected":1,"hidden":true},{"args":[[5,5,5],3],"expected":5,"hidden":true},{"args":[[2,2],4],"expected":1,"hidden":true},{"args":[[1000000000],2],"expected":500000000,"hidden":true},{"args":[[312884470],312884469],"expected":2,"hidden":true}]},{"id":"py-d18-search-rotated-array","day":18,"lang":"python","anchor":"Search in Rotated Sorted Array","cat":"BUILD","bounty":10,"title":"Search in Rotated Sorted Array","statement":"A sorted list of **distinct** integers was rotated at some point. For example `[0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7]` can become `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]`. It may also be rotated zero times.\n\nReturn the index of `target`, or `-1` if it is not in the list. Your solution should take O(log n) time.","starter":"def search_rotated(nums, target):\n    # Return the index of target in the rotated list, or -1.\n    pass\n","hints":["Plain binary search breaks because the whole list is not sorted. But after you pick a middle, at least one of the two halves is still sorted.","Compare nums[lo] with nums[mid]. If nums[lo] \u003c= nums[mid], the left half is sorted. Otherwise the right half is.","In the sorted half you can tell for sure whether the target is inside it by comparing with its two ends. If it is, search that half. If not, search the other one."],"approach":"**Trigger:** \"sorted, but rotated\" with an O(log n) requirement. **Pattern:** binary search that first asks \"which half is sorted?\" and then \"is the target in that half?\". **Complexity:** O(log n) time, O(1) space.\n\nUse `\u003c=` when you test whether the left half is sorted. With two elements, `mid` equals `lo`, the left half is that single element, and it is sorted. A strict `\u003c` sends you the wrong way on `[3, 1]`.\n\n```python\ndef search_rotated(nums, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while lo \u003c= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if nums[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        if nums[lo] \u003c= nums[mid]:            # the left half is sorted\n            if nums[lo] \u003c= target \u003c nums[mid]:\n                hi = mid - 1\n            else:\n                lo = mid + 1\n        else:                                # the right half is sorted\n            if nums[mid] \u003c target \u003c= nums[hi]:\n                lo = mid + 1\n            else:\n                hi = mid - 1\n    return -1\n```","kind":"func","cmp":"eq","fn":"search_rotated","signature":"def search_rotated(nums: list[int], target: int) -> int","cases":[{"args":[[4,5,6,7,0,1,2],0],"expected":4},{"args":[[4,5,6,7,0,1,2],3],"expected":-1},{"args":[[1],0],"expected":-1},{"args":[[3,1],1],"expected":1,"hidden":true},{"args":[[5,1,3],5],"expected":0,"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,3],3],"expected":1,"hidden":true},{"args":[[1,2,3,4],4],"expected":3,"hidden":true},{"args":[[4,5,6,7,0,1,2],6],"expected":2,"hidden":true},{"args":[[6,7,0,1,2,3,4,5],7],"expected":1,"hidden":true},{"args":[[2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,1],1],"expected":8,"hidden":true}]}];
